  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Risk of thunderstorms has decreased significantly\nfor tonight and Wednesdday.\n\nSurface dewpoints have dropped significantly across the area in\nthe past three hours behind a weak boundary that sparked of \ncouple of showers southeast of the Capital Beltway. In addition, \n00Z IAD sounding showed dry low levels below 700 mb on NW flow. \n\nCluster of thunderstorms over southern WV keeps tracking well\nsouth and the area of stratiform rain to the north keeps\nshrinking. Believe most of the area will be dry tonight with\nonly some high clouds. We'll be trimming PoPs for tonight into\ntomorrow. \n\nBased on current models, the second half of the night should\nlargely be dry. There may be some patchy fog if clearing occurs.\nLows will be in the 60s to lower 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers and thunderstorms remain possible at\ntimes Wednesday through Friday.\n\nThe west to east oriented cold front will remain near the area\nthrough Friday. The main question is whether we remain in west\nor northwest flow and more stable conditions, or if the richer\nmoisture is able to advect back in from the southwest. Periods\nof cloud cover could also be an issue that affects diurnal\ndestabilization. Many, but not all, models show a relative lull\non Wednesday as the greatest instability remains south and west\nof the area.\n\nOne, possibly two, waves of low pressure may track along the\nfront between Wednesday night and Friday. This could lift the\nfront northward, or at least provide a more notable source of\nforcing other than subtle shortwaves aloft. It's unclear how\nwell shear, instability, and forcing will overlap, but it is\npossible some stronger storms could develop if they do. ML/AI\nguidance has mixed signals on severe probabilities, but overall\nlower compared to Monday and today.\n\nTemperatures will likely be within several degrees of normal\nthrough Friday with elevated humidity levels.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Drier conditions anticipated heading into next \nweek, with a few showers & thunderstorms possible late this \nweekend. \n\nDry air moves in from the Great Lakes region at the start of the \nweekend as upper-level ridging builds over the lower Mississippi \nValley by Saturday. Temperatures remain slightly cooler than average \nduring this period with fewer precipitation chances overall. As \nthis high pressure builds towards the south, shortwave energy \nforming north of the ridge over the Great Lakes will track towards \nthe Mid-Atlantic Sunday and heading into Monday. This could produce \nchances for additional showers and thunderstorms, with most medium-\nrange severe outlooks currently producing rather marginal 5-15\npercent probabilities for severe thunderstorms. \n\nOnce this shortwave system passes through, zonal flow transitions \nover the region, reducing thunderstorm chances overall. Temperatures \nwill start to increase during this period as surface high pressure \ntransitions into the region.",
